-- title:
--   Corollary 3.1 — the discrete result at every lattice spacing
-- statement:
--   For $\vartheta > 0$, $\Lambda \ge 1$, $\alpha \in (0,2)$ and $R_0 > 0$ there
--   exist $\kappa \ge 1$ and $c \ge 1$ such that for every $\vartheta$-bounded
--   $\Gamma$, **every spacing $h > 0$**, every weight $\omega$ satisfying the
--   discrete bounds (4) on the lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ at threshold $R_0h$, every
--   $x_0$, every $R > 0$ and every $f$,
--
--   $$\sum_{\substack{x,y \in B(x_0,R) \cap h\mathbb{Z}^d \\ \|x-y\| > R_0h}}
--   \frac{(f(x)-f(y))^2}{\|x-y\|^{d+\alpha}}
--   \;\le\; c \sum_{\substack{x,y \in B(x_0,\kappa R) \cap h\mathbb{Z}^d \\ \|x-y\| > R_0h}}
--   (f(x)-f(y))^2\,\omega(x,y).$$
--
--   **$h$ is quantified after $\kappa$ and $c$**, so the constants are uniform in
--   the lattice spacing — that uniformity is the point of the rescaling.
--
--   Exactly where $h$ enters: it sets the lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$, and it scales
--   the separation threshold to $R_0h$, so pairs are summed when they are more than
--   $R_0$ *lattice spacings* apart. And nowhere else. In particular the kernel is
--   **not** rescaled — it is $\|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}$ for every $h$ — there is no
--   volume factor $h^d$, and the radii $R$, $\kappa R$ are not tied to $h$, so $R$
--   may be far smaller or far larger than the spacing.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Rescaling.lean#L173-L231

